<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Johannes Ockeghem</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1410–<a href="/years/1497">1497</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Okeghem</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Johannes Ockeghem was a Franco-Flemish composer and singer of the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>, the leading European composer between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guillaume-du-fay">Guillaume Du Fay</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josquin-des-prez">Josquin des Prez</a>. Probably born in Saint-Ghislain in the County of Hainaut, he is first recorded as a singer at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a>'s cathedral in 1443, and from around 1452 spent most of his career at the French royal court under Charles VII, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xi">Louis XI</a> and Charles VIII. Some 14 masses, five motets and 21 chansons are reliably attributed to him, among them the canon-based Missa prolationum and the earliest surviving polyphonic Requiem. His death in 1497 was lamented by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erasmus">Erasmus</a>, Jean Molinet and Josquin, who wrote Nymphes des bois for him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/johannes-ockeghem.jpg" alt="Johannes Ockeghem" width="330" height="458" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: anonymous · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anonymous_-_Portrait_of_a_Musician,_64.54.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ockeghem is believed to have been born in Saint-Ghislain in the County of Hainaut, in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>; documents of 1607 give &quot;Jan Hocquegam&quot; as a native of the town, which suggests that he was a native speaker of Picard. His birth date is unknown. Details of his early life are lacking, but like many composers of the time he probably began as a chorister. The first documented record of him is from June 1443, when he was employed as a &quot;left-hand choir singer&quot;, one who sang composed music, at the Onze-Lieve-Vrouwe cathedral in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a>. He probably sang there under Johannes Pullois.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Ockeghem probably studied with Gilles Binchois, and in any case was closely associated with him at the Burgundian court. Between 1446 and 1448 he served, with the composer Jean Cousin, at the court of Charles I, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a>, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moulins">Moulins</a>. Around 1452 he moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, where he served the French court as maestro di cappella, and he was also treasurer of the collegiate church of St Martin at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tours">Tours</a>. He served both Charles VII and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xi">Louis XI</a>, and held posts at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/notre-dame-de-paris">Notre Dame de Paris</a> and St Benoît.</p><p>In 1470 he travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> on a diplomatic mission for the king, which aimed to keep Spain out of an alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> and to arrange a marriage between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isabella-i-of-castile">Isabella I of Castile</a> and Charles, Duke of Guyenne.</p><p>He probably died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tours">Tours</a>, where he left a will, on 6 February 1497. Many laments were written on his death, among them poems by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erasmus">Erasmus</a> and Jean Molinet, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josquin-des-prez">Josquin des Prez</a>'s setting of Nymphes des bois.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Ockeghem's reliably attributed output is small for the length of his career: some 14 masses, an isolated Credo, five motets, a motet-chanson lamenting the death of Binchois in 1460, and 21 chansons. Thirteen of the masses are preserved in the Chigi codex, from around 1500. His Missa pro defunctis is the earliest surviving polyphonic Requiem. He used a cantus firmus in about half of his masses, and three of them, Ma maistresse, Fors seulement and Mi-mi, are based on chansons of his own.</p><p>The Missa prolationum consists entirely of mensuration canons, and the Missa cuiusvis toni is designed to be sung in any of the modes. His wide-ranging and rhythmically active bass lines were distinctive among Netherlandish composers. He influenced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josquin-des-prez">Josquin des Prez</a> and was known throughout <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> for his expressive music and technical skill.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Johannes Ockeghem” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The earliest surviving polyphonic Requiem, by <b>Johannes Ockeghem</b>, does not include it; the first settings to include it are by Engarandus Juvenis (1490) and Antoine Brumel (1516), and later ones by Charpentier, Delalande, Mozart, Berlioz, Verdi, Britten and Stravinsky.</blockquote><a href="/songs/dies-irae">Dies irae</a></li><li><blockquote>Taruskin argued that Busnois wrote the earliest, while other scholars favour Du Fay; Guillaume Faugues, Regis and <b>Johannes Ockeghem</b> may also have written settings in the 1450s.</blockquote><a href="/songs/lhomme-arme">L'homme armé (The Armed Man)</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tours">Tours</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=47.39,0.69&amp;year=1466" aria-label="Tours on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Johannes Ockeghem"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5040 6069h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5040 6069h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=47.39,0.69&amp;year=1466">What was here in 1466? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
